# Cameras

## Overview

`Fp3Camera` takes stills and streams video from the `:rear` and `:front`
cameras. The phone's camera image processor turns the sensors' raw data
into pictures in hardware: it demosaics, white balances, colour corrects
and scales every frame, so the CPU only encodes.

## Which sensors are fitted?

The camera modules can be swapped: a Fairphone 3 carries a Sony IMX363
at the rear and a Samsung S5K4H7YX at the front, a Fairphone 3+ a
Samsung S5KGM1SP and S5K3P9SP, and a phone can mix them. Ask which
sensor each slot holds, its sizes and whether it has a focus motor
(`:lens`):

```elixir
Fp3Camera.info(:rear)
```

```elixir
Fp3Camera.info(:front)
```

## Take a photo

A full-resolution still takes about a second:

```elixir
{:ok, jpeg} = Fp3Camera.snap_bytes(:rear)
Kino.Image.new(jpeg, :jpeg)
```

With 2×2 binning, the same view at a quarter of the pixels, it is quicker:

```elixir
{:ok, jpeg} = Fp3Camera.snap_bytes(:front, binned: true)
Kino.Image.new(jpeg, :jpeg)
```

## Choose the settings

Pick a camera and the panel shows the settings that camera takes: its
resolutions, the zoom it reaches at the chosen resolution and, on a
camera with a focus motor, the focus. Then press **Take photo**.

* **Zoom** is digital: the image processor crops the middle of the
  sensor's largest mode and scales it down to the resolution, so it
  reaches further at smaller resolutions.
* **White balance**, **exposure** and **gain** are automatic unless you
  set them. Exposure is capped by the frame time, about 33 ms.
* **Focus** goes from 0 (far away) to 1023 (very close). There is no
  autofocus yet, so the lens stays where you leave it.

```elixir
panel = Kino.Frame.new()
photo = Kino.Frame.new()
{:ok, last} = Agent.start_link(fn -> nil end)

auto_manual = [auto: "Automatic", manual: "Manual"]

form_for = fn values ->
  camera = values[:camera] || :rear
  {:ok, info} = Fp3Camera.info(camera)
  {:ok, sizes} = Fp3Camera.sizes(camera)
  size = if values[:size] in sizes, do: values[:size], else: hd(sizes)
  {:ok, max_zoom} = Fp3Camera.max_zoom(camera, size)

  zoom =
    if max_zoom > 1.0,
      do: [
        zoom:
          Kino.Input.range("Zoom (up to #{max_zoom}× at this resolution)",
            min: 1.0,
            max: max_zoom,
            step: 0.01,
            default: min(values[:zoom] || 1.0, max_zoom)
          )
      ],
      else: []

  focus =
    if info.lens,
      do: [
        focus:
          Kino.Input.range("Focus: 0 = far away, 1023 = very close",
            min: 0,
            max: 1023,
            step: 1,
            default: values[:focus] || 300
          )
      ],
      else: []

  fields =
    [
      camera:
        Kino.Input.select("Camera (#{info.sensor})", [rear: "Rear", front: "Front"],
          default: camera
        ),
      size:
        Kino.Input.select("Resolution", for({w, h} <- sizes, do: {{w, h}, "#{w}×#{h}"}),
          default: size
        )
    ] ++
      zoom ++
      [
        white_balance:
          Kino.Input.select("White balance", auto_manual, default: values[:white_balance] || :auto),
        red: Kino.Input.range("Manual white balance: red gain", min: 1.0, max: 3.99, step: 0.01, default: values[:red] || 2.0),
        blue: Kino.Input.range("Manual white balance: blue gain", min: 1.0, max: 3.99, step: 0.01, default: values[:blue] || 1.6),
        exposure: Kino.Input.select("Exposure", auto_manual, default: values[:exposure] || :auto),
        exposure_ms: Kino.Input.range("Manual exposure (ms)", min: 0.1, max: 33.0, step: 0.1, default: values[:exposure_ms] || 10.0),
        gain: Kino.Input.select("Gain", auto_manual, default: values[:gain] || :auto),
        gain_x: Kino.Input.range("Manual gain (×)", min: 1.0, max: 16.0, step: 0.1, default: values[:gain_x] || 1.0)
      ] ++ focus

  Kino.Control.form(fields, submit: "Take photo", report_changes: true)
end

settings_of = fn v ->
  [zoom: v[:zoom] || 1.0] ++
    if(v.white_balance == :manual, do: [wb: {v.red, 1.0, v.blue}], else: [awb: true]) ++
    if(v.exposure == :manual, do: [exposure: round(v.exposure_ms * 1000)], else: []) ++
    if(v.gain == :manual, do: [gain: v.gain_x], else: []) ++
    if(v[:focus], do: [focus: round(v.focus)], else: [])
end

show = fn show, values ->
  form = form_for.(values)
  Kino.Frame.render(panel, form)

  Kino.listen(form, fn
    %{type: :change, data: data} ->
      if data.camera != values[:camera] or data.size != values[:size], do: show.(show, data)

    %{type: :submit, data: data} ->
      {w, h} = data.size
      settings = settings_of.(data)
      Kino.Frame.render(photo, Kino.Text.new("Taking the photo..."))

      case Fp3Camera.snap_bytes(data.camera, [width: w, height: h] ++ settings) do
        {:ok, jpeg} ->
          Agent.update(last, fn _ -> {data.camera, settings} end)
          Kino.Frame.render(photo, Kino.Image.new(jpeg, :jpeg))

        {:error, reason} ->
          Kino.Frame.render(photo, Kino.Text.new("Not taken: #{inspect(reason)}"))
      end
  end)
end

show.(show, %{camera: :rear})
Kino.Layout.grid([panel, photo], columns: 1)
```

Happy with the result? Make the last photo's settings the default for
that camera's photos, and keep them across reboots:

```elixir
case Agent.get(last, & &1) do
  {camera, settings} ->
    Fp3Camera.configure({camera, :snap}, settings)
    Fp3Camera.save_config()

  nil ->
    "Take a photo with the panel first"
end
```

To go back to the built-in values:

```elixir
Fp3Camera.reset_config()
Fp3Camera.save_config()
```

## Watch it live

The camera's video, here in the notebook. Each frame is shown as soon as
it arrives and any that queued up behind it are dropped, so the picture
never lags behind the camera. Switch cameras while it runs; press
**Stop** to end it.

```elixir
camera = Kino.Input.select("Camera", rear: "Rear", front: "Front")
stop = Kino.Control.button("Stop")
video = Kino.Frame.new()
Kino.render(Kino.Layout.grid([camera, stop], columns: 2))
Kino.render(video)
Kino.Control.subscribe(camera, :camera)
Kino.Control.subscribe(stop, :stop)

# The camera being left is released just after, so a quick switch back
# may find it still busy for a moment
watch = fn watch, which, tries ->
  case Fp3Camera.subscribe(which, width: 1280, height: 720) do
    {:ok, feed} ->
      feed

    {:error, :busy} when tries > 0 ->
      Process.sleep(100)
      watch.(watch, which, tries - 1)
  end
end

newest = fn newest, frame ->
  receive do
    {:camera_frame, next} -> newest.(newest, next)
  after
    0 -> frame
  end
end

show = fn show, feed ->
  receive do
    {:stop, _event} ->
      Fp3Camera.unsubscribe(feed)

    {:camera, %{value: which}} ->
      Fp3Camera.unsubscribe(feed)
      show.(show, watch.(watch, which, 20))

    {:camera_frame, frame} ->
      %{data: nv12, width: w, height: h} = newest.(newest, frame)

      jpeg =
        nv12
        |> Evision.Mat.from_binary({:u, 8}, div(h * 3, 2), w, 1)
        |> Evision.cvtColor(Evision.Constant.cv_COLOR_YUV2BGR_NV12())
        |> then(&Evision.imencode(".jpg", &1, params: [Evision.Constant.cv_IMWRITE_JPEG_QUALITY(), 70]))

      Kino.Frame.render(video, Kino.Image.new(jpeg, :jpeg))
      show.(show, feed)
  end
end

show.(show, watch.(watch, Kino.Input.read(camera), 20))
```

## Stream video

The stream is H.264, encoded by the phone's video hardware, sent as raw
H.264 over TCP, which a browser can't play. On your laptop, open it
with `ffplay tcp://nerves.local:8888` (or `mpv`); the front camera
streams on port 8890. You can connect and disconnect at any time.
Streams are 1920x1080 at 30 fps and 8 Mbit/s unless you pass `:width`,
`:height`, `:framerate` or `:bitrate`, and they take the same settings
as photos, `:zoom` included.

```elixir
{:ok, stream} = Fp3Camera.start_stream(:rear)
Fp3Camera.streams()
```

Stop it when you're done:

```elixir
Fp3Camera.stop_stream(stream)
```
